{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "10 - Tools and MCP",
  "description": "Where the tools come from (Discovery and OpenAPI files), how tool servers start, caching, danger rules, and running a tool.",
  "nextTour": "11 - The approval pause",
  "steps": [
    {
      "directory": "backend/mcp_servers",
      "title": "What MCP is",
      "description": "**New idea — MCP (Model Context Protocol):** a standard way to offer tools to an AI. Each tool has a name, a description and an argument schema. An MCP *server* hosts the tools; an MCP *client* lists them and calls them.\n\n**How it works here:**\n- `providers/`: one MCP server per ad network\n- `utils.py`: builds servers from OpenAPI files\n- `curated_schemas.py`: hand-written, simpler arguments for some Google tools\n- `annotations.py`: tags and read/write labels for tools\n- `server.py`: a \"master\" server the agent doesn't use (last steps)\n\n**Why it matters:** the agent doesn't know AdMob's API. It only knows *there's a tool called X that does Y*. MCP is that contract.\n\n**Watch out:** `dependencies.py` has credential helpers that nothing calls (step 9)."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/mcp_servers/providers/admob.py",
      "line": 7,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 1,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 7,
          "character": 14
        }
      },
      "title": "AdMob in one line",
      "description": "**How it works:** the whole AdMob tool server is one call: build it from Google's Discovery file for the AdMob API (`admob_v1beta_discovery.json`).\n\n**Why it matters:** every AdMob operation Google publishes becomes a tool, without writing them one by one.\n\n**Watch out:** the factory lives in `admanager.py`, because Ad Manager was built first. AdMob reuses it."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/mcp_servers/providers/admanager.py",
      "line": 59,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 17,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 59,
          "character": 1
        }
      },
      "title": "Discovery file → tools",
      "description": "**New idea — Discovery file:** Google describes each API in a JSON file: resources (like `accounts.mediationGroups`), and methods on them (`list`, `create`, `patch`), with their paths and parameters.\n\n**How it works:** `process_resources` walks that tree. Each method becomes a tool named after its id, with dots changed to underscores: `accounts_mediationGroups_create`. If `curated_schemas.py` has a friendlier argument list for that tool, it's used; otherwise the tool takes a raw JSON body (`body_json`).\n\n**Why it matters:** this is how the agent gets every Google endpoint, and how tool names like the ones in tour 11 are made.\n\n**Watch out:** the folder nesting is only used to find methods; the tool name comes from the method id."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/mcp_servers/providers/admanager.py",
      "line": 195,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 142,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 195,
          "character": 4
        }
      },
      "title": "Writing Python code as text",
      "description": "**How it works:** for each tool, the code writes a whole Python function **as a string**: proper parameters, the URL path, the headers, and an HTTP call. The token comes from the `AUTH_TOKEN` environment variable and is sent as `Authorization: Bearer …`.\n\n**Why it matters:** MCP needs real functions with real parameters to describe each tool. Writing them as text is how a file full of endpoints becomes hundreds of proper functions.\n\n**Watch out:** two consequences:\n- the generated code isn't in the repo, so you can't read a tool's code directly\n- errors are **returned** as data (`{\"error\": …}`), not raised. Google's errors reach the model as a normal result, so the retry logic in step 14 never sees them"
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/mcp_servers/providers/admanager.py",
      "line": 212,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 210,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 212,
          "character": 90
        }
      },
      "title": "Turning text into a tool",
      "description": "**New idea — exec:** `exec` runs a string as Python code. Here it defines the function, which is then registered as an MCP tool with `mcp_instance.tool(...)`.\n\n**How it works:** the shared HTTP client and helpers are passed in through `local_namespace`, so the generated code can use them.\n\n**Why it matters:** `exec` is usually avoided, but the input here is Google's own trusted file, not user text.\n\n**Watch out:** if one tool fails to build, it's just printed and skipped. A missing tool won't stop the server."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/mcp_servers/utils.py",
      "line": 103,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 62,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 103,
          "character": 15
        }
      },
      "title": "The other seven networks",
      "description": "**How it works:** AppLovin, Unity, Mintegral, Liftoff, InMobi, Pangle and DT Exchange each have an OpenAPI file in `backend/api_specs/`. `FastMCP.from_openapi` turns every endpoint into a tool, using an HTTP client with the network's base URL.\n\n**Why it matters:** each of those provider files is just two lines (look at `providers/applovin.py`).\n\n**Watch out:** the token is read **once**, when the server starts, and only from `AUTH_TOKEN` (next step)."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/tools/loader.py",
      "line": 44,
