        }
      },
      "title": "Who gets a credential",
      "description": "**How it works:** the loader fetches an OAuth token only for the networks in this map: AdMob (`admob`) and Ad Manager (`gam`). That token becomes the tool server's `AUTH_TOKEN`.\n\n**Why it matters:** only these two networks can act on a user's account.\n\n**Watch out:** the other seven tool servers start **without any credential**. `dependencies.py` has helpers to fetch their API keys, but nothing calls them. Their tools exist, but they can't log in. If asked how many networks fully work, the honest answer is two."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/tools/loader.py",
      "line": 297,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 261,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 297,
          "character": 24
        }
      },
      "title": "Starting a tool server",
      "description": "**How it works:** for each network, the config says: run `python -m mcp_servers.providers.<network>` from the backend folder, talk over `stdio`, and pass an environment with your user id, the right import path, the virtual environment, and your token.\n\n**New idea — stdio:** the agent and the tool server talk through the child process's standard input and output. No ports, no network.\n\n**Why it matters:** each network runs in its own process, with only that user's token in it.\n\n**Watch out:** the venv and `PYTHONPATH` lines exist because the child process doesn't inherit the parent's import tweaks. Missing them gives *module not found* errors."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/tools/loader.py",
      "line": 219,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 161,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 219,
          "character": 16
        }
      },
      "title": "Getting your token",
      "description": "**How it works:** first check an in-memory cache. If it's not there, POST to the API's `/api/providers/internal/token` (tour 05) with the shared secret, and cache the answer.\n\n**Why it matters:** the agent never stores your Google tokens. It asks the API, which decrypts and refreshes them.\n\n**Watch out:** the cache keeps a token for a full hour, but the API may hand over a token with only a few minutes left (it refreshes when under 5 minutes remain). So the agent can keep using an expired token for a while, and Google answers with an auth error."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/tools/loader.py",
      "line": 63,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 48,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 63,
          "character": 76
        }
      },
      "title": "Two caches",
      "description": "**How it works:**\n- tokens: cached for 1 hour per user, provider and team\n- MCP clients and their tool lists: cached for 5 minutes per user, team and service\n\nThe key includes the user, so one user's tools (with their token inside) are never reused for another.\n\n**Why it matters:** starting tool servers is slow. The cache means your next message within 5 minutes reuses them.\n\n**Watch out:** both caches live in the agent's memory, so they're per server and empty after a restart."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/tools/loader.py",
      "line": 403,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 357,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 403,
          "character": 80
        }
      },
      "title": "Loading the tool list",
      "description": "**How it works:** use the cache if it's fresh. Otherwise build the config, create a `MultiServerMCPClient`, ask the servers for their tools, and cache the result. The tools are also added to the tool registry (next step).\n\n**Why it matters:** this is what the specialist calls in tour 09 to get its tools.\n\n**Watch out:** these tool objects are used twice: to describe tools to the model, and to run them in step 13."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/tools/registry.py",
      "line": 49,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 30,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 49,
          "character": 2
        }
      },
      "title": "What counts as dangerous",
      "description": "**How it works:** a tool name matching `create_`, `update_`, `delete_`, `patch_` (at the start or the end) is a write. If the name doesn't match, the first 100 characters of the description are checked for words like *create*, *update*, *delete* or *add*.\n\n**Why it matters:** dangerous tools pause for your approval (tour 11). This list decides which ones.\n\n**Watch out:** the description check looks for substrings, so *add* also matches *address* and *additional*. That errs on the safe side: harmless tools may ask for approval, never the other way round."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/tools/loader.py",
      "line": 533,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 474,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 533,
          "character": 43
        }
      },
      "title": "Running a tool",
      "description": "**How it works:** find the tool by name: in tools passed in, then in the cache, then by starting the servers again, and finally in the *all networks* set. Then `tool.ainvoke(args)` sends the call through MCP to the server process, which calls the real API.\n\n**Why it matters:** this is the last hop before AdMob or Ad Manager.\n\n**Watch out:** if the cache expired between the specialist's choice and this call, the servers are started again. That's slower, but it works."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/nodes/tool_executor.py",
      "line": 1599,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 1574,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 1599,
          "character": 22
        }
      },
      "title": "Retries",
      "description": "**How it works:** up to 3 tries. Between tries it waits 1 second, then 2. Errors mentioning *timeout*, *connection*, *429* or *5xx* are retried; login, permission and other 4xx errors aren't.\n\n**Why it matters:** brief network hiccups don't fail your request.\n\n**Watch out:** as step 4 showed, the Google tools return HTTP errors as data instead of raising them. So these retries mostly catch problems talking to the tool server itself."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/nodes/tool_executor.py",
      "line": 687,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 669,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 687,
          "character": 58
        }
      },
      "title": "Errors the AI can fix",
      "description": "**How it works:** a failed tool's error is rewritten as **ISSUE**, **TO FIX** and **ATTEMPTED** sections. For a bad ad unit id, it says to call `accounts_adUnits_list` first; for prices, it reminds the model that 1 USD = 1,000,000 micros.\n\n**Why it matters:** the model reads this as the tool's result and usually corrects itself on the next pass.\n\n**Watch out:** it recognises errors by matching words in the text. A new wording from an API falls through to generic advice."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/mcp_servers/server.py",
